ailed we can't confirm  whether the shelling that we've heard  this morning was either incoming or  outgoing but obviously if there is this  road or areas on this road that is being  shelled that would suggest indeed it is  russian shelling  earlier ukraine's president accused nato  of giving russia the green light to  further bombard and kill ukrainians  because it was refusing to establish a  no-fly zone russia's foreign minister  has hit back in zelinski's speech  accusing him of wanting to provoke  further conflict  if zelensky's so unhappy with nato not  interfering then that means he's hoping  to resolve this conflict by involving  nato it means he's not hearing  statements from european capitals saying  they won't get involved it means he  wants to provoke a conflict between nato  and russia  that's the mood he's in this mood  indicates that he doesn't need  negotiations with us but that may change  because of course he's a moody person  natasha butler is following developments  in the western ukrainian city of levees  the russian foreign minister didn't have  much  good to say of course about the  ukrainian president or ukrainian  authorities ukrainian authorities he  said  were preventing uh that uh evacuation of  citizens from mario paul this town uh on  the sea of azov in the south of ukraine  uh a ceasefire is supposed to be taking  place there between uh russian troops  and uh ukrainian forces but uh that  doesn't seem to be happening uh but  lavrov is saying that that is the fault  of the ukrainian side of ukrainian  troops uh who are not allowing that  humanitarian corridor and ceasefire to  take place he also uh talked about uh  zielinski vladimir silenski the  ukrainian president accusing him of  trying to provoke uh nato possibly  trying to provoke nato into into  becoming part of this conflict uh  zelensky earlier he did call on nato to  try and impose a no-fly zone over the  country he says that would help stop  russian jets and planes uh from  bombarding cities and towns across the  country nato though has made it very  clear that they do not intend to do that  jen stoltenberg the head of nato uh  saying that there won't be  any way in which the alliance is going  to send jets to shoot down or use any  form of artillery to shoot down russian  planes over ukrainian airspace because  that would basically draw the military  alliance into this war something that  western allies say they do not want to  be part of  ukraine's president zolensky is set to  speak virtually to the entire u.s senate  shortly let's find out more and bring in  heidi joe castro joining us from  washington dc so tell us more about this  heidi  well doreen before congress right now is  a pending aid package to ukraine that  totals some 10 billion dollars it's for  military assistance humanitarian as well  as economic and it does have bipartisan  support and it could move as quickly as  being signed into law next week so today  the zoom appearance by president  zielinski before the full senate is the  ukrainian leader's chance to make a  direct appeal about how that money can  be best used to help his country but  what zielinski is expected to ask for of  course is  what he says he most needs right now the  u.s involvement in establishing a no-fly  zone and it is the one thing that the  bidet administration has been consistent  and clear in saying that ukraine cannot  have the reasoning for that again just  as we heard in the case with european  allies is that the u.s does not want to  escalate this into a direct armed  conflict between u.s forces that may  potentially have to shoot down russian  planes to enforce a no-fly zone and that  is a red line  from the white house as far as congress  is concerned there have been one or two  voices the vast minority saying that the  u.s should think about doing a no-fly  zone in ukraine but the vast majority of  congress has been opposed to that idea  as well and i'm sure president zielinski  this